                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">No. 128</seg>
                </opener>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi>
                </head>
                <p>On April 8, Prime Minister Pholien arrived in Washington for a scheduled meeting
                    with Dean Acheson on the following day. In the period since his arrival in New
                    York with his wife on March 27, he had visited the United Nations and dined with
                    Ambassador Austin, seen West Point, toured industrial plants in Pittsburgh and
                    Detroit, and viewed the installations and training facilities at
                    Wright-Patterson Air Force Base in Ohio, Connelly Air Force Base in Texas where
                    Belgian cadets were training, and Fort Benning Army installation in Georgia.</p>
                <p>The Prime Minister’s proposed visit to the United States was first mentioned, and
                    favorably presented, in telegram 1400 from Brussels, February 28. Telegram 1435
                    from Brussels, March 6, added that it would be well if Pholien could “visit
                    industrial center Middle West where he could obtain inspiration from size of our
                    defense effort.” These telegrams and other documents related to Departments of
                    State and Defense scheduling of this trip are in file 755.13.</p>
            </div>
